  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Shepwan- &apos;MUMA A YI DA MU&apos; Project</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Hoomsen Women Farmers Multi-Purpose Cooperative Society is an informal group of women farmers based in Shepan community, in Shendamlocal government area of Plateau State; they are popularly known as Shepwan Women Farmers. Under their previous empowerment grant with Voice, they were able to organize better, set up a safe space for engagement and collectively secured a farmland- a feat that would have been difficult to achieve if they approached it individually because of cultural limitations against women.

Current project would like to expand the membership of the women&#x2019;s cooperative group, strengthen their advocacy skills and seek for inclusion of women in decision making at community level. They will also be sustaining the safe space set up under the previous project as this was one of the most impactful aspects of the previous project.

Thus, the overall goal of the project is to strengthen the voices of Shepwan Women farmers and position them better for political inclusion within their local authority and to achieve this, the following actions are planned:
&#x2022; Mobilize and sensitize more women from other communities within the local government for one unified, stronger voice
&#x2022; Build the capacity (confidence) of the women so that they will be individually empowered and to enlighten them on the need to come together and make their voices hear
&#x2022; Advocacy &lt;(&gt;&amp;&lt;)&gt; sensitization of traditional gatekeepers to help them understand the need for the voice of women to be heard within the traditional council.
Support the political ambitions of any woman within the local authority by organizing and mobilizing support during any election whether at community level or at local government level
&#x2022; Support &lt;(&gt;&amp;&lt;)&gt; facilitate continuation of monthly safe space meeting of the women as they leverage on the strength of one another.

The change that this project seeks is a Shepwan community where contributions of women are appreciated and respected, Voices of womencount in decision making; because the women are more coordinated, strengthened and courageous to take these spaces.</narrative>
  </description>
  <participating-org role="4" type="24">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Hoosem Women Farmers Multi-Purpose</narrative>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">The Hoomsen Women Farmers Co-operative Society is a co-operative society registered by two groups of women farmers- &apos;the Home#sun and the Na#antukurum&apos; women farmers shepwan.  They both started as an association of 20 women farmers  membership each, located in a small farming village called Na#mess in Shepwan community of  Shendam Local Government Area of Plateau State. The group saw the needto come together and support each other at individual and group level through communal farming activities sometimes for a fee or asa support to members as is culturally practiced .They are simply a group of women farming at a small scale to cater for the needs of their families. They mainly farm rice and other cash crops such as cowpea, beans, yams etc.

These two groups of women farmers were later encouraged by and supported by a Non-Governmental Organization called Country Women Association of Nigeria (COWAN) between June - July, 2003, to merged since they have common interest, so as to have a more structured group; and they later got registered asa women farmers# Cooperative organization in July 2008 with the Nigerian Agricultural development bank in Shendam LGA.

The Cooperative is currently made up of a total number of 40 fully registered members. As a registered cooperative group they cultivate jointly, harvest, store and later sell the harvest in the market and some of the proceed is ploughed into the Cooperative account to serve as loan to members during the lean farming season that is characterized with lack of food in some households. They also take these loans to buy farming inputs and meet up with basic family needs when they are hit by economic hardship.

Currently, their major challenge is getting a large farm land for the use of their members.They have been getting land on loan from the men in the community because the tradition does not allow them as women, to own land. However, with their cooperative status, they can acquire land. This land acquisition is the focus of the grant that they arecurrently receiving from Voice.</narrative>
  </participating-org>
